<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angkor Thom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> Thom was a walled royal city built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> in the late twelfth century. At its centre stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> temple, famous for its many carved stone faces. It is one of the major monuments of the Khmer Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-thom.jpg" alt="Angkor Thom" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. … · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Thom_(II).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Angkor Thom, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> for Great City, was the last and most enduring capital of the Khmer Empire. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> established it as the centre of his massive building programme, and one inscription found in the city calls the king the groom and the city his bride. It was not the first Khmer capital on the site. Yasodharapura, dating from about three centuries earlier, lay slightly further northwest, and Angkor Thom overlapped parts of it. The Baphuon, a former state temple, and Phimeanakas, which was incorporated into the Royal Palace, stand within its walls. The name Angkor Thom came into use from the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The city covers 9 square kilometres on the west bank of the Siem Reap River. Its laterite walls, 8 metres high and flanked by a moat, are each 3 kilometres long. A gate stands at each cardinal point, with roads leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>, Jayavarman's state temple, at the centre. Archaeologists interpret the walls and moat as the mountains and oceans around the Bayon's Mount Meru.</p><p>The gate towers, 23 metres high, carry faces like those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>, which may represent the king, the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, guardians of the cardinal points, or some combination. Each causeway across the moat has devas on one side and asuras on the other, holding a naga in a tug-of-war that appears to refer to the Churning of the Sea of Milk. Most of the enclosed land held secular buildings, of which nothing remains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The last temple known to have been built there, Mangalartha, was dedicated in 1295. The city is believed to have supported 80,000 to 150,000 people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> sacked it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmers</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponhea-yat">Ponhea Yat</a> moved their capital southeast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>. Angkor Thom was abandoned before 1609, when an early western visitor described an uninhabited city. The Poem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, composed in Khmer verse in 1622, describes its beauty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Angkor Thom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…sack of Angkor by Cham forces in 1177 exposed the empire's vulnerability, but Jayavarman VII drove out the invaders, defeating them in a celebrated naval battle on the Tonle Sap, enclosed his new capital of <b>Angkor Thom</b> within massive walls and moat, and subjugated Champa itself in 1203.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Chams sacked Angkor in 1178, Jayavarman VII restored the empire, built a new capital at <b>Angkor Thom</b> with the Bayon as its state temple, and dedicated it to Buddhism, encouraged by his wife Indradevi, a devout Mahayana Buddhist.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In the reign of Jayavarman VII alone Ta Prohm, Preah Khan, Banteay Kdei and Neak Pean were built, beside the Bayon at the centre of <b>Angkor Thom</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
